    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sch-1-sec-193",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "193 ACMA may grant exemptions for trial services",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sch-1-sec-193"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.46",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The ACMA may exempt a specified person from all or any of the\nobligations imposed on the person under Division 1 in so far as\nthose obligations relate to a kind of telecommunications service\nthat is a trial service.\n\n(2) The ACMA must not grant an exemption unless the ACMA, after\nconsulting any interception agencies that the ACMA considers\nappropriate, is satisfied that the exemption is unlikely to create a\nrisk to national security or law enforcement.\n\n(3) The exemption must be in writing.\n\n(4) The exemption may be:\n\n(a) unconditional; or\n\n(b) subject to such conditions as are specified in the exemption.\n\n(5) An exemption given under subsection (1) is not a legislative\ninstrument.\n\nCo-operation with agencies  Chapter 5\nInterception capability plans  Part 5-4\n\nSection 195\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The ACMA may exempt a specified person from all or any of the\nobligations imposed on the person under Division 1 in so far as\nthose obligations relate to a kind of telecommunications service\nthat is a trial service.\n\n(2) The ACMA must not grant an exemption unless the ACMA, after\nconsulting any interception agencies that the ACMA considers\nappropriate, is satisfied that the exemption is unlikely to create a\nrisk to national security or law enforcement.\n\n(3) The exemption must be in writing.\n\n(4) The exemption may be:\n\n(a) unconditional; or\n\n(b) subject to such conditions as are specified in the exemption.\n\n(5) An exemption given under subsection (1) is not a legislative\ninstrument.\n\nCo-operation with agencies  Chapter 5\nInterception capability plans  Part 5-4\n\nSection 195\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sch-1-sec-193-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sch-1-sec-193-ss-2"
