    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sch-1-sec-298",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "298 Protection of persons—control order declared to be void",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sch-1-sec-298"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.65",
      "values:originalText": "(1) If:\n\n(a) a warrant was issued on the basis that an interim control\norder was in force; and\n\n(b) a court subsequently declares the interim control order to be\nvoid;\na criminal proceeding does not lie against a person in respect of\nanything done, or omitted to be done, in good faith by the person:\n\n(c) in the purported execution of the warrant; or\n\n(d) in the purported exercise of a power, or the purported\nperformance of a function or duty, in a case where the\npurported exercise of the power, or the purported\nperformance of the function or duty, is consequential on the\nwarrant.\n\n(2) Subsection (1) does not apply to a thing done, or omitted to be\ndone, at a particular time if, at that time, the person knew, or ought\nreasonably to have known, of the declaration.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) If:\n\n(a) a warrant was issued on the basis that an interim control\norder was in force; and\n\n(b) a court subsequently declares the interim control order to be\nvoid;\na criminal proceeding does not lie against a person in respect of\nanything done, or omitted to be done, in good faith by the person:\n\n(c) in the purported execution of the warrant; or\n\n(d) in the purported exercise of a power, or the purported\nperformance of a function or duty, in a case where the\npurported exercise of the power, or the purported\nperformance of the function or duty, is consequential on the\nwarrant.\n\n(2) Subsection (1) does not apply to a thing done, or omitted to be\ndone, at a particular time if, at that time, the person knew, or ought\nreasonably to have known, of the declaration.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sch-1-sec-298-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sch-1-sec-298-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "4213d47302c77fd2439cd482b563a3ac46448bcaa6216edb9a5a058d207bb17b"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sch-1-sec-298",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "298 Protection of persons—control order declared to be void",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 65,
      "values:originalText": "(1) If:\n\n(a) a warrant was issued on the basis that an interim control\norder was in force; and\n\n(b) a court subsequently declares the interim control order to be\nvoid;\na criminal proceeding does not lie against a person in respect of\nanything done, or omitted to be done, in good faith by the person:\n\n(c) in the purported execution of the warrant; or\n\n(d) in the purported exercise of a power, or the purported\nperformance of a function or duty, in a case where the\npurported exercise of the power, or the purported\nperformance of the function or duty, is consequential on the\nwarrant.\n\n(2) Subsection (1) does not apply to a thing done, or omitted to be\ndone, at a particular time if, at that time, the person knew, or ought\nreasonably to have known, of the declaration."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sch-1-sec-298-ss-1",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "298(1) Protection of persons—control order declared to be void",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sch-1-sec-298-ss-1"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.65",
      "values:originalText": "(1) If:\n\n(a) a warrant was issued on the basis that an interim control\norder was in force; and\n\n(b) a court subsequently declares the interim control order to be\nvoid;\na criminal proceeding does not lie against a person in respect of\nanything done, or omitted to be done, in good faith by the person:\n\n(c) in the purported execution of the warrant; or\n\n(d) in the purported exercise of a power, or the purported\nperformance of a function or duty, in a case where the\npurported exercise of the power, or the purported\nperformance of the function or duty, is consequential on the\nwarrant.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) If:\n\n(a) a warrant was issued on the basis that an interim control\norder was in force; and\n\n(b) a court subsequently declares the interim control order to be\nvoid;\na criminal proceeding does not lie against a person in respect of\nanything done, or omitted to be done, in good faith by the person:\n\n(c) in the purported execution of the warrant; or\n\n(d) in the purported exercise of a power, or the purported\nperformance of a function or duty, in a case where the\npurported exercise of the power, or the purported\nperformance of the function or duty, is consequential on the\nwarrant.",
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sch-1-sec-298"
      },
      "cml:asserts": [
