    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sch-1-sec-121",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "121 Prescribed communications provider may object to",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sch-1-sec-121"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.221",
      "values:originalText": "international production order\n\n(1) If an international production order is given to the prescribed\ncommunications provider to whom the order is directed, the\nprovider may, by written notice given to the Australian Designated\nAuthority, object to the order on the grounds that the order does\nnot comply with the designated international agreement nominated\nin the application for the order.\n\n(2) The notice must:\n\n(a) be given to the Australian Designated Authority within a\nreasonable time after the international production order is\ngiven to the prescribed communications provider; and\n\n(b) set out the reasons why the provider considers that the order\ndoes not comply with the designated international agreement\nnominated in the application for the order.\n\nSchedule 1  International production orders",
      "skos:definition": "international production order\n\n(1) If an international production order is given to the prescribed\ncommunications provider to whom the order is directed, the\nprovider may, by written notice given to the Australian Designated\nAuthority, object to the order on the grounds that the order does\nnot comply with the designated international agreement nominated\nin the application for the order.\n\n(2) The notice must:\n\n(a) be given to the Australian Designated Authority within a\nreasonable time after the international production order is\ngiven to the prescribed communications provider; and\n\n(b) set out the reasons why the provider considers that the order\ndoes not comply with the designated international agreement\nnominated in the application for the order.\n\nSchedule 1  International production orders",
      "cml:hasPart": [
