    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sch-1-sec-126",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "126 Civil penalty provision—enforcement",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sch-1-sec-126"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.226",
      "values:originalText": "Enforceable civil penalty provision\n\n(1) A civil penalty provision in this Part is enforceable under Part 4 of\nthe Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014.\nNote:\nPart 4 of the Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014\nallows a civil penalty provision to be enforced by obtaining an order\nfor a person to pay a pecuniary penalty for the contravention of the\nprovision.\nAuthorised applicant\n\n(2) For the purposes of Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers (Standard\nProvisions) Act 2014, a Communications Access Coordinator is an\nauthorised applicant in relation to a civil penalty provision in this\nPart.\nRelevant court\n\n(3) For the purposes of Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers (Standard\nProvisions) Act 2014, the Federal Court of Australia and the\nFederal Circuit and Family Court of Australia are relevant courts in\nrelation to a civil penalty provision in this Part.\nPenalty for a body corporate\n\n(4) Paragraph 82(5)(a) of the Regulatory Powers (Standard\nProvisions) Act 2014, as it applies in relation to a civil penalty\nprovision in this Part, has effect as if “5 times” were omitted and\n“200 times” were substituted.\nExtra-territorial application\n\n(5) Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014,\nas it applies in relation to a civil penalty provision in this Part,\nextends to acts, omissions, matters and things outside Australia.\n\nInternational production orders  Schedule 1\nReporting and record-keeping requirements  Part 9\nIntroduction  Division 1\n\nClause 127\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026",
      "skos:definition": "Enforceable civil penalty provision\n\n(1) A civil penalty provision in this Part is enforceable under Part 4 of\nthe Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014.\nNote:\nPart 4 of the Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014\nallows a civil penalty provision to be enforced by obtaining an order\nfor a person to pay a pecuniary penalty for the contravention of the\nprovision.\nAuthorised applicant\n\n(2) For the purposes of Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers (Standard\nProvisions) Act 2014, a Communications Access Coordinator is an\nauthorised applicant in relation to a civil penalty provision in this\nPart.\nRelevant court\n\n(3) For the purposes of Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers (Standard\nProvisions) Act 2014, the Federal Court of Australia and the\nFederal Circuit and Family Court of Australia are relevant courts in\nrelation to a civil penalty provision in this Part.\nPenalty for a body corporate\n\n(4) Paragraph 82(5)(a) of the Regulatory Powers (Standard\nProvisions) Act 2014, as it applies in relation to a civil penalty\nprovision in this Part, has effect as if “5 times” were omitted and\n“200 times” were substituted.\nExtra-territorial application\n\n(5) Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014,\nas it applies in relation to a civil penalty provision in this Part,\nextends to acts, omissions, matters and things outside Australia.\n\nInternational production orders  Schedule 1\nReporting and record-keeping requirements  Part 9\nIntroduction  Division 1\n\nClause 127\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026",
